      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Sleep deprivation and obesity, are associated with neurocognitive impairments. Effects of sleep deprivation and obesity on cognition are unknown, and the cognitive long-term effects of improvement of sleep have not been prospectively assessed in short sleeping, obese individuals."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To characterize neurocognitive functions and assess its reversibility."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Prospective cohort study."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "Tertiary Referral Research Clinical Center."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENTS",
          "content": "A cohort of 121 short-sleeping (<6.5 h/night) obese (BMI 30-55 kg/m(2)) men and pre-menopausal women."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INTERVENTION",
          "content": "Sleep extension (468±88 days) with life-style modifications."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MEASUREMENTS",
          "content": "Neurocognitive functions, sleep quality and sleep duration."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "At baseline, 44% of the individuals had an impaired global deficit score (t-score 0-39). Impaired global deficit score was associated with worse subjective sleep quality (p\u200a=\u200a0.02), and lower urinary dopamine levels (p\u200a=\u200a0.001). Memory was impaired in 33%; attention in 35%; motor skills in 42%; and executive function in 51% of individuals. At the final evaluation (N\u200a=\u200a74), subjective sleep quality improved by 24% (p<0.001), self-reported sleep duration increased by 11% by questionnaires (p<0.001) and by 4% by diaries (p\u200a=\u200a0.04), and daytime sleepiness tended to improve (p\u200a=\u200a0.10). Global cognitive function and attention improved by 7% and 10%, respectively (both p\u200a=\u200a0.001), and memory and executive functions tended to improve (p\u200a=\u200a0.07 and p\u200a=\u200a0.06). Serum cortisol increased by 17% (p\u200a=\u200a0.02). In a multivariate mixed model, subjective sleep quality and sleep efficiency, urinary free cortisol and dopamine and plasma total ghrelin accounted for 1/5 of the variability in global cognitive function."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "LIMITATIONS",
          "content": "Drop-out rate."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Chronically sleep-deprived obese individuals exhibit substantial neurocognitive deficits that are partially reversible upon improvement of sleep in a non-pharmacological way. These findings have clinical implications for large segments of the US population."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "TRAIL REGISTRATION",
          "content": "www.ClinicalTrials.gov NCT00261898. NIDDK protocol 06-DK-0036."
        }
      ]},
